  "id": "Compound::DB00679 - Gene::3362",
  "verbalization": "Thioridazine appears to exert its effects through a shared mechanism involving the binding of Htr6, a protein implicated in various neurological and physiological processes. The evidence suggests that Thioridazine, as an antipsychotic agent, induces symptoms such as parotid swelling and choreoathetoid movements, which are mediated by other antipsychotics like perphenazine and fluphenazine. These latter drugs bind to Htr6, indicating a potential role for Htr6 in the pathophysiology of antipsychotic-induced side effects. The recurring involvement of Htr6 in multiple pathways, coupled with the shared classification of Thioridazine and its analogs as organonitrogen heterocyclic compounds and antipsychotics, suggests that Htr6 may be a key target in the pharmacological action of these drugs. The integration of these findings highlights a possible dependency on Htr6 for the therapeutic or adverse effects of Thioridazine, positioning it within a broader network of interactions involving antipsychotic agents and their downstream targets.",
